# The annotations of tape block 3, the machine-code loader at $FFDC-$FFFD (34 bytes): the source of
# src/loader/loader.asm (../../kit/mkgame.py --part src/loader/loader.json). Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.

@module loader ; The machine-code loader: the screen and the game by LD-BYTES, the headers before them skipped
@about Entered by the BASIC's RANDOMIZE USR 65500 (c1, frame 1017). It asks the ROM's LD-BYTES ($0556) for a block of flag $FF twice: 6912 bytes to the screen, then 40000 bytes to $6338. A standard header (flag $00) before each fails the flag test, LD-BYTES returns with carry clear, and the loader asks again: so the two headers on the tape (blocks 4 and 6) are read and dropped. It never returns: the game takes over at $D6B0 (c1, frame 14 100 T 17 980). The game's 40000 bytes end at $FF77, below this code, so the load does not overwrite the loader while it runs.

$FFDC c Loader ; Drop USR's return, load the screen and the game, enter the game
> USR leaves the address of the ROM's statement return on the stack; POP AF drops it, the game never returns to BASIC.
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$FFDD LoadScreen ; IX = $4000, DE = 6912, A = $FF (a data block), carry set (load): LD-BYTES
$FFEC LoadGame ; IX = $6338, DE = 40000: LD-BYTES again, again until it succeeds
$FFFB EnterGame ; JP $D6B0: the game's entry (GameEntry)
@equ $0556 LdBytes ; the ROM's LD-BYTES: IX the address, DE the length, A the flag, carry set to load; carry clear on return when the block failed
@equ $D6B0 GameEntry ; the game's entry (src/game/entry.asm)
@equ $4000 Screen 6912 at $FFDD ; the screen
@equ $6338 GameStart at $FFEC ; the game's first byte (src/game)
